<style>
body { font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif; font-size: 12pt; line-height: 1.4; margin: 20px; }
.header { text-align: center; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 2px solid #000; padding-bottom: 10px; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class='header'>
    <h2>Lotus Academy</h2>
    <h3>Unit Test — Physics (Class 9 A)</h3>
</div>
<table width='100%' style='margin-bottom:15px;font-weight:bold;'>
    <tr>
        <td>Time Allowed: 1 Hours</td>
        <td align='right'>Maximum Marks: 30</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Teacher: Admin</td>
        <td align='right'>Date: 20/08/2026</td>
    </tr>
</table>
<hr>
<h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Choose the correct answer for each of the following questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Which of the following is a physical quantity that has both magnitude and direction?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Distance</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Speed</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Displacement</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Time</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> The slope of a distance-time graph represents which physical quantity?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Acceleration</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Speed</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Displacement</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Retardation</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> The SI unit of acceleration is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) m&middot;s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) m/s<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) cm/s<sup>2</sup></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> Area under a velocity-time graph represents:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Acceleration</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Speed</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Displacement</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Time elapsed</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> To convert a speed from km/h to m/s, we multiply it by:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">18</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">5</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">5</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">18</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">5</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">60</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">1000</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">60</span></span></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q6.</strong> When a body moves with uniform velocity, its acceleration is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Positive</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Negative</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Zero</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Variable</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q7.</strong> If the velocity-time graph of an object is a straight line parallel to the time axis, the motion of the body is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Accelerated</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Retarded</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Uniform velocity</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) At rest</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q8.</strong> A body moves in a circular path of radius r. What is its displacement after completing one full revolution?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 2&pi; r</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) &pi; r</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Zero</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 2r</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section B: Assertion-Reason</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: For questions 9 and 10, select the correct option out of the four choices (a), (b), (c), and (d).</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q9.</strong> Assertion (A): The displacement of an object can be zero even if the distance covered by it is not zero.
Reason (R): Displacement is a vector quantity that depends on the initial and final positions of the object.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is NOT the correct explanation of Assertion.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Assertion is true but Reason is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Assertion is false but Reason is true.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q10.</strong> Assertion (A): The slope of a velocity-time graph gives the acceleration of a moving body.
Reason (R): Acceleration is defined as the rate of change of displacement with time.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is NOT the correct explanation of Assertion.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Assertion is true but Reason is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Assertion is false but Reason is true.</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section C: Very Short Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions briefly.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q11.</strong> Differentiate between scalar and vector quantities with one example of each related to motion.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q12.</strong> State any two key differences between speed and velocity.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q13.</strong> A car accelerates uniformly from 18 km/h to 36 km/h in 5 seconds. Calculate its acceleration in m/s<sup>2</sup>.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q14.</strong> Write the three fundamental equations of motion for a body moving with uniform acceleration.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q15.</strong> What is meant by retardation or deceleration? Give a practical example.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section D: Short Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions with proper steps and explanations.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q16.</strong> Derive the second equation of motion, s = ut + <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">1</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2</span></span>at<sup>2</sup>, using a graphical method.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q17.</strong> A train starting from rest attains a velocity of 72 km/h in 5 minutes. Assuming that the acceleration is uniform, find:
(i) The acceleration of the train in m/s<sup>2</sup>.
(ii) The distance traveled by the train during this time interval.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section E: Long Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following comprehensive questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q18.</strong> Draw neat and labeled distance-time graphs for the following conditions and describe the nature of motion represented by each graph:</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>A body at rest.</li><li>A body moving with uniform speed.</li><li>A body moving with non-uniform (increasing) speed.</li></ol><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q19.</strong> A ball is thrown vertically upwards with an initial velocity of 20 m/s. (Take acceleration due to gravity, g = 10 m/s<sup>2</sup>)</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>Calculate the maximum height reached by the ball.</li><li>Find the time taken by the ball to reach the highest point.</li><li>What will be the total time taken by the ball to return back to the thrower's hand?</li></ol>
</body>
</html>